From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dsound: Build with SSE floating point math.

The 32-bit build of dsound does all of its floating point work, the
resampling filter included, on the x87 FPU, because the i386 compiler
defaults to x87 math. Rosetta translates x87 code far more slowly than
SSE, so resampling a 44.1 kHz buffer for a 48 kHz device takes a large
share of the 10 ms mixer period, and two such buffers at once overrun
it: the mixer falls behind and the output stutters and crackles. The
64-bit build already uses SSE and is not affected.

Compile the module with SSE2 scalar math. Every CPU Rosetta presents
has SSE2, and the computed values are the same single precision floats
the 64-bit build produces. The mixer thread already flushes denormals,
which now covers the 32-bit build as well.

---
diff --git a/dlls/dsound/Makefile.in b/dlls/dsound/Makefile.in
--- a/dlls/dsound/Makefile.in
+++ b/dlls/dsound/Makefile.in
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 MODULE    = dsound.dll
 IMPORTLIB = dsound
 IMPORTS   = dxguid uuid winmm ole32 advapi32 user32
+EXTRADEFS = -msse2 -mfpmath=sse
 
 SOURCES = \
 	buffer.c \
